for the environment secretary george  eustis is here it's good to see you this  morning look this report was  commissioned by the home secretary does  that mean the government is going to act  on the recommendations  well obviously pretty patel will now  look at uh these recommendations but i  think you know this report does  highlight some differences between  police forces and i think in these sorts  of situations violence against women is  a serious problem domestic violence has  also been a problem for some time now  and what we really need to do is to  learn from those police forces that are  addressing this well and i think report  highlights that some of them are doing  far better than others  and that actually try and replicate  those approaches that uh that work in  parts of the country it's it it's not  purely a police issue though is it i  mean this report says three quarters of  domestic abuse cases are closed without  even a suspect being charged and not all  but some of that is down to to victims  sort of withdrawing the complaint if you  like i mean there's a wider societal  issue here isn't there yes well it is  and i know in devon and cornwall uh that  the police force where i come from we  have some real problems with uh domestic  violence in some of our communities and  actually often some of the things that  can work quite well are  um you know involvement to to help the  partners that get angry and get violent  and actually to try and and help you  know defuse and manage some of those  stressful situations in the home which  often are behind some of these things so  you get you know you can get those uh  those other sorts of interventions that  can help deal um with with problems of  domestic violence i mean i know there  there is already a scheme in place to  help sort of  children primary school children learn  about healthy relationships but if if  children are being brought up in a home  where there is  violence or aggression  can that can they be targeted in a way  to to make sure they don't get caught in  that spiral  well you know we have lots of family  support workers and and interventions  and obviously social services and local  authorities it's not just those that are  under a very close watch they also have  family support workers that try to do  this but you know a chaotic home  environment where there's lots of anger  lots of violence it isn't a great  environment for those children and there  are some good programs that i've seen  where you get interventions to try to  defuse those types of situations um get  both parents in that relationship to  you know to learn to sort of manage  their anger issues and that can actually  deal with some of the sort of lower  level um uh domestic violence of course  you then get still those very serious  cases and that's when the police do have  to drive it through and take a  prosecution forward yeah i mean in terms  of what the government can do to signal  its intent what about the istanbul  convention i mean that was an  international agreement to  to to come to  to sort of common standards in how we  deal with violence against women and  girls it was open for signatures in 2011  10 years ago 45 countries have signed  the uk still hasn't i mean do we need to  do that well look i think the fact that  pretty patel um you know commissioned  this report asked for this piece of work  shows the seriousness with which she  takes this issue  and i think what we really want is that  uh you know domestic policy response to  deal with this problem learn from those  police forces that are addressing uh the  problem and doing well and replicate  that in the others and i'm sure that's  exactly what priti patel will want to be  doing with police forces around the  country okay look we're going to have a  lot more on that throughout the course  of the morning but in the meantime can  we talk about  covid and travel as someone who's  finally going on a holiday next month  this is particularly interesting to me i  have to say what is happening are we  expecting an announcement on these sort  of pcr tests as you return to the uk is  that going to be happening today well my  understanding is no decisions have  actually been taken yet although i  understand there may be a meeting today  to review this we regularly  review those travel restrictions  obviously we took um a very important  step a bit earlier this summer when we  removed the need to quarantine for those  coming from ambulance countries that was  a really big step forward but we have  retained the need for testing and that's  really so that we can pick up any  variance of concern through that pcr  test but look i know um that this has  been raised by the travel industry that  they think some of that testing may be  unnecessary may be onerous the  government will be uh listening to that  and obviously the uh the coveted  subcommittee of cabinet that uh decides  these things will be considering that  probably later today i mean there seems  to be a pretty clear indication and i  don't quite know where it all comes from  but  lots of rumors flying about that pcrs  could be scrapped for lateral tests  which obviously are cheaper and easier  is is that something you've heard  well um i haven't heard because i'm not  on that particular subcommittee that  deals with this and obviously you know  they will want to consider all of the  evidence before taking a final decision  i mean the rationale for the pcr test  is that you can do sort of genome  sequencing of variants and you can  therefore  detect possible variants of concern and  the the difficulty with the lateral flow  test although it's cheaper and it's  simpler to do is is that it's not able  to pick up those variants so that's the  rationale and been the rationale so far  for making sure that we keep some of  that pcr testing in place  but when you look at the situation that  our our code infection rates are higher  than they are in a lot of mainland  europe particularly even though our  vaccination system is is is pretty good  um  but with that in mind i mean it it does  raise questions doesn't mean even the  health secretary made the point that he  thought this this need to be needed to  be seriously looked at  uh yes so i did say that we would um you  know look at this he doesn't want to  have unnecessary testing in place but i  think he also recognizes the value of  those pcr tests so you know there's a  there's a discussion to be had about  this as i said we've already made that  big step forward of ending the need for  quarantine from people returning from  ambulance countries that was a game  changer if we can do uh further  refinements on testing then obviously  we'll look at that to you know reduce  those burdens but we do um also need to  be you know mindful and vigilant against  new variants coming in we have done  incredibly well with our vaccination  program it enabled us to open things up  again and the the vaccination that we've  done is preventing those  hospitalizations and keeping that  mortality right down uh but we what we  don't want is to end up with a variant  that can evade the vaccine and that's  what we've got to remain vigilant about  it it is reaching a critical point  though for the travel industry isn't it  obviously it's been a dreadful couple of  years and we're now easing off into a  sort of quieter season so  if there was ever a right time to do  this it's now isn't it well look it's  been a very very uh difficult time for  the travel industry we absolutely  recognize that that's why we've done all  we can to  you know have those easements in place  reduce the restrictions as quickly as we  can  but you know arguably the biggest threat  to the travel industry is that we do get  another variant that manages to get  around the vaccine that the vaccine  can't deal with then we're into another  full lockdown and that's not what we  want that's why we've taken this  cautiously step by step because we want  each step we take to be irreversible i  know you're making us wait for any  announcement and that's fair enough to  to an extent but there is also talk of  scrapping the amber and just having red  and green now  that may seem sensible to a lot of  people the problem is that's following  on from what the labour party was  demanding  months ago  well i actually still think there's the  rationale  for amber and has been because we have  gained by having those pcr tests on  people returning because it has given us  extra protection extra surveillance  against those potential variants of  concern the redless countries remain uh  the highest risks but but we think  there's been that uh rationale for the  ambulance and by the way i'm not waiting  for the announcement i'm waiting for a  decision that i understand has not yet  been discussed and perhaps will be later  today okay um can we talk reshuffle  because obviously you've kept your job  uh which is very good news to you and  you've got the first meeting of the new  cabinet what about nine o'clock this  morning something like that yes um  what has the prime minister been trying  to achieve with this reshuffle because  he i mean he certainly hasn't created a  cabinet with a sort of single ideology  has he  uh no but i i think you know the  conservative party has always been a  broad church and any government any  prime minister will want to bring  together a cabinet of all the talents  and we'll want to make sure you've got  lots of different perspectives  represented that's how you get a good  debate in cabinet and that's exactly  what he's done and i think really what  he's uh seeking to do with this  reshuffle is get the team in place that  he wants to you know deliver a post  pandemic the recovery of our country to  build back better build back greener get  our economy going again  get wages up get people in better paid  jobs improve skills and this is his big  focus over the  remaining part of this parliament and  he's assembled his team to do that so i  mean very much what a focus on on a  domestic agenda and  and within that then how big a role is  michael gove going to play housing  secretary  and yet the the sort of leveling up  agenda seems to fall within his remit  now that's  going to be crucial moving forward for  the conservatives isn't it yes well look  i worked very closely with michael gove  when he was in my own department in in  defra when i was a minister there  he's got a formidable intellect um very  good at problem solving very good at  getting to grip with detail quickly um  there are some very important reforms  we've got on planning where we want to  simplify the process but make sure we've  got the right uh protections in place  make sure we build aesthetically uh you  know beautiful housing around the  country and not the usual sort of  identicate things that we've become used  to so i think he's in a very uh good  position to take those reforms forward  and also  he retains that brief around uh you know  the wider uk and that's a passion of his  but is it right that at this point in in  a government cycle that we are i mean it  seems pretty clear you're gearing up for  the next election that could be 2023 it  could be 2024. oliver dowden seems to be  pretty convinced that now you need to  get on the election footing is that your  understanding well look um i think what  oliver dowden said is we should be ready  for an election whenever it might come  um and that's what every party chairman  will always do their task is to make  sure that the party is ready but for  cabinet as a whole uh our priority is to  deliver we've still got uh several years  left of this parliament and we've got a  lot that we want to achieve there's  going to be a lot of challenges that  need addressing uh post this pandemic  that's going to be our absolute focus  and can you do that within even if  you've got sort of two or three years  left can you deliver everything that was  promised back in 2019  well look the um you know the pandemic  wasn't in anyone's manifesto as the  prime minister said so we've already had  to take uh difficult but necessary and  right choices on things like the the  health and social care levy that we've  introduced to to deal with that huge  backlog in the nhs and also to get our  social care system on a  a firmer more viable footing so we're  willing to take those uh difficult  decisions but yeah we also want to crack  on and deliver many of those things that  were in our manifesto delivering for the  environment which is a very much a focus  for me with an environment bill going  through now improving uh our approach on  things like animal welfare so i've got  uh bills like that that i'm focusing on  other departments will have their own  priorities that will also manifest their  commitments okay minister good to talk  to you thank you thank you very much

